        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-emerald-400 font-mono">1.</span> Mathematical Anatomy of Perpetual Futures Funding Mechanics
        </h2>
        <p>
          Unlike traditional futures contracts (such as CME quarterly index or commodity futures), <strong>perpetual contracts do not possess a fixed calendar expiration date</strong>. Without an expiration date enforcing convergence to spot price via physical or cash settlement at expiry, cryptocurrency derivatives exchanges implement a continuous financial tether: the <em>Perpetual Funding Rate</em>.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          The funding rate is calculated by comparing the perpetual contract mark price ($P_&#123;mark&#125;$) against the underlying external spot index ($P_&#123;index&#125;$). When bullish market participants push the mark price above the index ($P_&#123;mark&#125; &gt; P_&#123;index&#125;$), long traders pay short traders every epoch (typically 8 hours or 1 hour). Conversely, during bearish discount regimes ($P_&#123;mark&#125; &lt; P_&#123;index&#125;$), shorts pay longs.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          On major institutional exchanges (Binance, Bybit, OKX, Hyperliquid), the funding rate $F$ is defined through two distinct components: the <strong>Premium Index</strong> ($P$) and the <strong>Interest Rate Component</strong> ($I$):
        </p>
        <div class="my-4 p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/80 border border-slate-800 font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm text-emerald-300">
          <div class="text-slate-400 mb-1">// Mathematical Formulation of 8-Hour Funding Rate</div>
          <div>Premium Index: P = [Max(0, ImpactBid - SpotIndex) - Max(0, SpotIndex - ImpactAsk)] / SpotIndex</div>
          <div class="mt-2">Funding Rate: F = P + Clamp(I - P, -0.05%, +0.05%)</div>
          <div class="mt-1 text-slate-400 text-xs">Where I = 0.01% (standard fixed 8-hour interest benchmark).</div>
        </div>
      </section>

