          To model the financial return of a delta-neutral cash-and-carry position, consider an allocated capital pool $C_0$ split equally between spot inventory and short derivative collateral.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          Let $F_t$ represent the funding rate collected at discrete epoch $t \in [1, N]$. For standard 8-hour settlements:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 mt-2 text-slate-200">
          <li>Daily settlement count: $k = 3$</li>
          <li>Annual settlement epochs: $N = 365 \times 3 = 1095$</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-3">
          The <strong>Simple Annualized Percentage Rate (APR)</strong> assuming an average 8-hour funding rate $\bar&#123;F&#125;_&#123;8h&#125;$ is:
        </p>
        <div class="my-3 p-3 bg-slate-950 border border-slate-800 rounded-lg font-mono text-emerald-400 text-sm">
          APR = \bar&#123;F&#125;_&#123;8h&#125; \times 1095
        </div>
        <p class="mt-3">
          When funding income is automatically swept into the margin pool or re-hedged into the spot leg every 8 hours, returns compound exponentially. The <strong>Compounded Annual Percentage Yield (APY)</strong> is:
        </p>
        <div class="my-3 p-3 bg-slate-950 border border-slate-800 rounded-lg font-mono text-cyan-400 text-sm">
          APY = (1 + \bar&#123;F&#125;_&#123;8h&#125;)^&#123;1095&#125; - 1
        </div>

