          <span class="text-emerald-400 font-mono">3.</span> Liquidation Hedging &amp; Margin Buffer Equations
        </h2>
        <p>
          While the net portfolio delta is mathematically zero:
        </p>
        <div class="my-2 p-3 bg-slate-950 border border-slate-800 rounded font-mono text-xs text-slate-300">
          \Delta_&#123;net&#125; = \Delta_&#123;spot&#125; + \Delta_&#123;perp&#125; = (+1.00) + (-1.00) = 0.00
        </div>
        <p class="mt-3">
          <strong>The asymmetric liquidation trap</strong> represents the fatal risk in sloppy execution. The spot leg can appreciate infinitely without liquidation risk. However, the short perpetual leg carries an explicit liquidation boundary. If the underlying price surges aggressively before margin rebalancing can occur, the exchange will forcefully liquidate the short leg, locking in severe losses and leaving the desk naked long.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          The liquidation price $P_&#123;liq&#125;$ of an isolated short perpetual position is derived from the maintenance margin condition:
        </p>
        <div class="my-4 p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/80 border border-slate-800 font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm text-cyan-300">
